# Mutation cases for "a test that spawns sce-codegen declares the feature
# that builds it".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_target_that_needs_the_binary_says_so.cases`.
#
# `sce-codegen` is `required-features = ["cli"]`. Cargo hands a test target
# `CARGO_BIN_EXE_sce-codegen` whether or not that feature is on — the value is
# where the binary WOULD go — so a target that spawns it without declaring the
# feature compiles either way and then dies on `NotFound`. Unless an earlier
# build left one at that path, in which case it passes, and the suite reports
# a green it did not earn.
#
# Measured 2026-09-02: 25 of the 53 binary-driven targets carried no
# declaration. In a clean target directory `cargo test -p sce-build --test
# rust_derive_ssot` failed 14 of its 16 cases on `spawn sce-codegen:
# NotFound`; the same command in the working tree passed, because
# `target/debug/sce-codegen` was on disk from an unrelated build. Two lanes
# had written the defect down in prose — `rust-workspace-tests.yml` named four
# offenders and `workspace-tests.sh` counted fifteen gated targets — and both
# figures were stale, which is what a count in prose does.
#
# The fix costs something, and the second half of the gate is that cost: cargo
# drops an unmet-features target WITHOUT building it and WITHOUT reporting a
# skip, so a declaration turns a loud run-time death into a silent absence.
# Hence the third question — does every command that reaches such a target
# turn the feature on — and the floor under its sweeping arm.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test cli_feature_gating
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs
# Read at run time: the manifest is the declaration under judgement, and the
# two gate scripts are commands that must enable the feature.
mutation_runtime_targets sce-build/Cargo.toml scripts/gates/drift-suites.sh
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/gates/clippy.sh

# The defect itself, put back one target at a time. `rust_derive_ssot` is the
# one whose clean-target run was measured above.
mutation_case "a target that spawns the binary loses its declaration" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/Cargo.toml",
     'name = "rust_derive_ssot"\npath = "tests/rust_derive_ssot.rs"\nrequired-features = ["cli"]',
     'name = "rust_derive_ssot"\npath = "tests/rust_derive_ssot.rs"')
PY

# The other direction. A declaration cargo cannot justify is not harmless: the
# target stops running the moment a lane spells its features differently, and
# nothing says so. `integration_stem_registration` reaches no binary and is
# named by `tree-hygiene.sh`, which passes `--features cli,ffi` — so this case
# can only redden the manifest question, not the lane one.
mutation_case "a target that needs no binary is declared as if it did" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/Cargo.toml",
     '[[test]]\nname = "addr2sce_pc"',
     '[[test]]\nname = "integration_stem_registration"\n'
     'path = "tests/integration_stem_registration.rs"\n'
     'required-features = ["cli"]\n\n[[test]]\nname = "addr2sce_pc"')
PY

# The cost of the fix, made real. `b9_drift_detection` is gated, and this lane
# names it; without the flag cargo drops the target and the gate reports
# success for a suite it never built.
mutation_case "a lane running a gated target by name drops the flag" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/drift-suites.sh",
     "cargo test -p sce-build --features cli --test b9_drift_detection",
     "cargo test -p sce-build --test b9_drift_detection")
PY

# A scanner that finds nothing agrees with everything, and both manifest
# questions would pass on a blinded reader — the undeclared set and the
# spurious set are both empty when the population is empty. The floor is what
# refuses that.
mutation_case "the reader stops recognising the macro that reaches the binary" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     'format!("env!(\\"{BIN_ENV}\\")")',
     'format!("env!(\\"{BIN_ENV}-no-such-var\\")")')
PY

# Prose that explains a mechanism must not be counted as the mechanism. This
# file's own header spells the macro call out in a comment, so a reader that
# stops stripping comments finds it there and reports the gate itself as an
# undeclared binary-driven target — the reading `common::rust_source` exists
# to prevent, arriving here through the one file guaranteed to talk about it.
#
# The stripper is called by its full path in the gate for this case's sake:
# with a `use` line the deletion also retires the import, `-D warnings` refuses
# the tree, and the case reports INCONCLUSIVE instead of a red. That is how
# this one first came back.
mutation_case "the reader counts the prose that explains the macro" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     "without_spaces(&common::rust_source::code_only(text)).contains(&call)",
     "without_spaces(text).contains(&call)")
PY

# The same reading on the shell/YAML side, from the other direction: comments
# QUOTE commands. `rust-workspace-tests.yml`'s header recounts the bare
# `cargo test --workspace` that used to run these targets with no binary — a
# sentence about a command nobody runs — and a scan that reads it finds a lane
# sweeping the package without the flag.
#
# The anchor is the strip, not the sentence, so the case stays aimed if the
# header is rewritten. If a rewrite ever removes every quoted command this
# case goes SURVIVED rather than silent, which is the outcome to act on.
mutation_case "the scan reads the comments that quote a command" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     "if trimmed.starts_with('#') {",
     'if trimmed.starts_with("# no such prefix") {')
PY

# The lane question has two arms and they fail differently. Blinding the
# sweeping arm leaves the naming arm finding everything `tree-hygiene.sh`
# lists, so the count of examined commands stays positive and no offender is
# reported: a scan that stopped looking, wearing the shape of a pass. Only the
# arm's own floor refuses it.
mutation_case "the sweeping arm stops recognising a workspace run" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     '    let reaches_package = cmd.contains("--workspace")\n'
     '        || cmd.contains("-p sce-build")\n'
     '        || cmd.contains("--package sce-build");',
     '    let reaches_package = cmd.contains("--workspace-no-such-flag")\n'
     '        || cmd.contains("-p sce-build-no-such-package")\n'
     '        || cmd.contains("--package sce-build-no-such-package");')
PY

# The regression the declaration itself caused, and the three places a
# reader can lose it. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` asks for
# every target the FEATURE SET allows, so declaring the features took 25
# integration targets out of lint coverage with no message at all —
# measured on the commit that declared them: 135 of this package's targets
# linted, none of the 53 gated ones, back to 188 with the flag.
#
# ⚠ This case SURVIVED on its first run and that is what it is worth. The
# gate script hands `sce_gate_fail` a label repeating the command, joined
# to it by a continuation backslash, so removing the flag from the command
# left it standing in the label and `features_of` still found it. The
# reader now splits at `||`, `&&`, `;` and `|`, and THIS case is what holds
# that split in place — a case removing the split alone would survive,
# because with the real flag present both halves answer the same. One case
# doing both jobs, as with the comment strip above.
mutation_case "the lint lane drops the flag the declaration made it need" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/clippy.sh",
     "cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --features cli -- -D warnings",
     "cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings")
PY

# ...and the two ways the reader stops seeing that lane at all. Neither is
# caught by the offender list — with the flag in place there is no offender
# to lose — so both land on the lint floor, which is why that floor exists
# separately from the sweeping one.
mutation_case "the scan reads only the subcommand it was born reading" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     'const TARGET_SUBCOMMANDS: &[&str] = &["cargo test", "cargo clippy", "cargo check", "cargo build"];',
     'const TARGET_SUBCOMMANDS: &[&str] = &["cargo test"];')
PY

mutation_case "asking for all targets stops counting as reaching them" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/cli_feature_gating.rs",
     '    cmd.contains("cargo test") || asks_for_tests',
     '    cmd.contains("cargo test")')
PY

# Two mutations deliberately NOT written, for two different reasons:
#
#   * Removing `without_spaces` cannot be observed. Every one of the 53 call
#     sites fits on a line, so the normalised text and the raw text answer
#     alike and the mutation is a no-op. It is there for the rustfmt line
#     break that has not happened yet, and a case that cannot fail is not
#     evidence.
#   * Widening `sweeps_the_package`'s `--test` guard DOES redden, and that is
#     why it is not here: the red is a false report rather than a defect
#     found. `measure-scope-obligation.sh` names one ungated target, so cargo
#     builds that target alone and there is no gated target in the run to be
#     dropped from it. A case whose red says something untrue would teach the
#     next reader to widen the guard back.
